## The surface - everything AUTO-LOADED into an LLM context, nothing else

In scope: root `CLAUDE.md`, EVERY nested `CLAUDE.md` at ANY depth, `CLAUDE.local.md`, `.claude/rules/*.md`
(single level - CC loads rules non-recursively), convention files, the `AGENTS.md` family,
`.claude/agents/**` at ANY depth (CC scans subfolders such as `agents/review/`), `.claude/skills/**` (SKILL.md AND every
`references/*.md`), and BOTH memory kinds: the session memory dir `{MEMORY_DIR}` and the PER-AGENT memory stores -
a tree holding one store per agent, loaded only while that agent runs (which kind loads for whom:
`references/memory-guide.md`). If a file can silently enter a model's context, it is memory; if it cannot, it is not.

A tracked file OUTSIDE this tree that an always-loaded instruction file cites is not part of the surface: it
enters the editable set only at `{REACH}` = `REFS`. Being git-tracked never removes an instruction file from the
surface - most of the surface is tracked. Both memory kinds sit INSIDE the surface and are swept at BOTH reaches:
reach widens the editable set OUTWARD to cited references, and its value `MEMORY` names that axis, never a batch.

Batches are **DISJOINT BY CONSTRUCTION** - no file appears in two batches - so parallel edits can never collide.
An overlapping batch table is a defect, not a style choice.

EVERY memory home is its OWN batch row - the memory dir one, each per-agent store one (Phase 1 enumerates them;
split a store tree further only when one batch is too big). A memory home is never folded into a CLAUDE.md, rules,
agents or skills batch. ONE exception, so the rule-5 salvage stays inside one batch and never edits a foreign
file: a store classified ORPHAN joins the batch owning the SAME agent's LIVE store. That agent has no live store
anywhere -> the orphan keeps its own row and its survivors have no home: REPORT them, remove nothing.

