## Runtime workflow seats - edited with the whole instruction set in view

A runtime seat is an agent a PROGRAM spawns - a workflow file, an orchestrator script, a skill naming a
`subagent_type` - as opposed to an agent a human routes to by keyword. Its effective prompt is the UNION of four
sources, and only one of them is the agent file:

| # | Source | Where it lives |
|---|--------|----------------|
| 1 | the agent body | `.claude/agents/<name>.md` |
| 2 | the call-site prompt | the text the caller passes when it spawns the seat |
| 3 | interpolated constants | model-policy tables, preamble/relay blocks, schema fragments the caller splices in |
| 4 | every OTHER call site | the same seat spawned from a second place, with different text |

<!-- BLOCK: | Seat | Agent file | Call sites (file:line) | Constant blocks it receives | -->
{RUNTIME_SEAT_ROSTER}

Editing one source blind to the other three is how a contradiction enters. Enumerate all four and read them
together BEFORE touching a seat's file; then:

| Rule | Detail |
|------|--------|
| Facts first | judge on correctness, consistency and contradiction - never on prose taste |
| Contradiction | quote BOTH texts and REPORT it; reconcile silently only when one side names something that no longer exists - then fix that side and say which |
| Contract lines | never compress a line a call site depends on: a return-schema field, a tool name, a command, an id, a threshold, a status string |
| One writer | a seat's file belongs to exactly one batch (disjointness already guarantees it); its checker re-reads every call site above |

